WEBVTT

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.819
My name is Jordan and I'm from Indiana.
我叫乔丹，来自印第安纳州。

00:00:03.819 --> 00:00:06.854
Last summer I started my third viewing of how I met your mother.
去年夏天，我开始第三次重温《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

00:00:06.854 --> 00:00:13.256
My first girlfriend had just broken up with me, and the show felt like the only thing that could get me through those first three days.
我的初女友刚和我分手，这部剧感觉像是唯一能陪我度过最初那三天的事物。

00:00:13.256 --> 00:00:15.911
I remember watching Return of the Shirt very distinctly.
我清楚地记得自己看《一件衬衫的回归》那一集时的情景。

00:00:15.911 --> 00:00:18.425
I was practically shouting at Ted the entire episode.
那一整集我几乎都在冲着泰德大喊大叫。

00:00:18.425 --> 00:00:27.482
While the topic hit a little close to home at that point, I realized how interesting it was to be able to view this show with that new perspective, one of someone who had been broken up with.
虽然当时这个话题有点戳中我的痛处，但我意识到，能以这种全新的视角——一个刚经历分手的人的视角——来欣赏这部剧，是一件多么有趣的事。

00:00:27.482 --> 00:00:29.332
I think that's the beauty of this show.
我想这就是这部剧的魅力所在。

00:00:29.332 --> 00:00:32.130
I get something new out of it every single time I watch it.
每次我看，都能从中获得新的感悟。

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:53.659
I'll be soon in New York City, when I see you, please permit me to tell you everything of city.
我很快就要去纽约市了，等见到你时，请允许我和你讲讲关于这座城市的一切。

00:00:53.659 --> 00:00:55.820
Hello, welcome to how we made your mother.
大家好，欢迎来到《我们是如何创造老爸老妈的》。

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:57.060
I'm Josh Radner.
我是乔什·拉德诺。

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:02.659
I am joined as ever by my dear friend and how I met your mother co-creator Craig Thomas.
和往常一样，陪伴我的有我的挚友、《老爸老妈浪漫史》的联合创作者克雷格·托马斯。

00:01:02.659 --> 00:01:03.659
Hello, Craig.
你好，克雷格。

00:01:03.659 --> 00:01:04.659
Hey, Josh.
嘿，乔什。

00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:07.579
We're talking about episode four, season one, return of the shirt.
我们要聊的是第一季第四集，《一件衬衫的回归》。

00:01:07.579 --> 00:01:09.659
This episode aired when, Alec.
这一集是什么时候播出的，亚历克。

00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:12.340
On October 10th, 2005.
在2005年10月10日。

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:18.939
October 10th, 2005, nearly 20 years ago, it's so long ago, it's so long ago.
2005年10月10日，快20年前了，太久远了，真的太久远了。

00:01:18.939 --> 00:01:19.939
I can't handle it.
我简直不敢面对。

00:01:19.939 --> 00:01:20.939
Okay.
好的。

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:21.939
Who do we have joining us today?
今天和我们一起连线的都有谁？

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:24.780
I'm very excited to have our guest today appropriately enough.
我非常高兴能请到今天的嘉宾，可以说是再合适不过了。

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.780
We have the writer of this episode, return the shirt.
我们请到了这一集《一件衬衫的回归》的编剧。

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:36.019
In the entire amount of amateur mother, there were only two writers who were there from day one, from the pilot, like the pilot punch up session, right?
在整部《老爸老妈浪漫史》中，从第一天、从试播集起就在的编剧只有两位，就像试播集润色会议那样，对吧？

00:01:36.019 --> 00:01:39.659
Hired onto the show on the pilot, there are 208 episodes, two writers.
从试播集就被雇用到剧组，一共208集，只有两位编剧。

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:46.980
Chris Harris, another awesome all-timer who got to it at some point, and the other one is our guest today.
克里斯·哈里斯，另一位在某个阶段加入的传奇人物，而另一位就是我们今天的嘉宾。

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.140
The writer of the show, Courtney Kang.
本剧的编剧，考特尼·康。

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.140
Courtney Kang.
考特尼·康。

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:51.140
Courtney Kang.
考特尼·康。

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.140
Hi, Courtney.
嗨，考特尼。

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:53.140
Yeah.
耶。

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.379
Thank you so much for having me.
非常感谢邀请我来。

00:01:55.379 --> 00:01:58.579
Oh my God, we're so happy you're here.
天哪，你能来我们太高兴了。

00:01:58.579 --> 00:02:00.579
Thanks for giving us a decade of your life, by the way.
顺便说一句，谢谢你把十年的青春奉献给我们。

00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:01.579
Thanks for that.
谢谢你。

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:02.579
Yeah.
对。

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:03.579
Thanks for that decade.
谢谢你那十年。

00:02:03.579 --> 00:02:04.579
A full decade.
整整十年。

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:05.739
Yes.
是的。

00:02:05.739 --> 00:02:06.739
What?
什么？

00:02:06.739 --> 00:02:07.739
Like, did we carry?
比如说，我们都过30岁了吗？

00:02:07.739 --> 00:02:09.580
I don't think we got to 40, right?
我记得我们还没到40岁吧？

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:12.819
You were both on our 30s and almost got, I think I'm a little older than you.
我们当时都30多岁，差不多快到……我觉得我比你稍大一点。

00:02:12.819 --> 00:02:18.099
I think it'd be just shy of 40 when we did our 30s on how I met your mother, right?
我觉得拍《老爸老妈浪漫史》的时候，我们30多岁，离40岁只差一点点，对吧？

00:02:18.099 --> 00:02:19.419
That's pretty much it.
差不多就是这样。

00:02:19.419 --> 00:02:23.259
I always tell people I started the show in my late 20s.
我总是跟别人说我加入剧组时快30岁。

00:02:23.259 --> 00:02:25.900
I was, I think I was like 28 or 29.
我当时大概28或29岁吧。

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.500
I started the show like single.
我进剧组时还是单身。

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:36.219
I had just started dating my now husband and I left the show a mother of two like staring down 40.
我刚开始跟我现在的丈夫约会，而我离开剧组时已经是两个孩子的妈妈了，正面对着40岁的到来。

00:02:36.219 --> 00:03:04.500
So it was those like, And I feel like what was great about it is all of us, we're kind of in that sort of same age bracket and we were going through what the gang was going through and that was totally informing the stories, like Lilian Marshall getting married, starting to have children, career stuff, like we were going through it with the gang and I think it was like, and we were all going through it.
所以就是那些……而且我觉得最棒的是，我们大家基本上都在同一个年龄段，我们经历着主角团正在经历的事情，而这完全为故事提供了灵感，比如莉莉和马修结婚、开始生孩子、职业上的事，我们和主角团一起经历着这一切，而且我觉得，当时我们所有人都在经历这些。

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.259
You know, the cast was going through their things, the writers, the crew, everybody.
你知道，演员们在收拾他们的东西，编剧们，剧组人员，所有人都在。

00:03:08.259 --> 00:03:14.460
It wasn't like the Hollywood, you know, 28 year olds playing high school students kind of like silliness.
这不像好莱坞那种，你知道的，28岁的人去演高中生的那种无聊把戏。

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:20.659
Like we were, we were in the pocket of the era that we were, we were showing, you know?
就像我们，我们正处于我们所展现的那个时代的内核中，你知道吗？

00:03:20.659 --> 00:03:24.979
And the writing, the writing staff wasn't like these grizzled veterans who'd been doing it lots of years.
而且剧本创作团队也不像那种干了很多年的老油条。

00:03:24.979 --> 00:03:29.259
And if this show doesn't work out, we'll go on to some other show like this was our shot, right?
觉得如果这部剧不行了，我们还可以去搞别的剧，这就好像是我们的绝佳机会，对吧？

00:03:29.259 --> 00:03:30.259
This was our show.
这就是我们的剧。

00:03:30.259 --> 00:03:31.259
This was going to be our show.
这将会是属于我们的剧。

00:03:31.259 --> 00:03:42.821
We're the same age as the cast, Courtney, you were a little younger than me, but we were basically contemporaries and it felt like we were all sharing material and sharing stories and we all spoke the same language and I think that was like so key.
我们和演员们年龄相仿，考妮，你比我小一点，但我们基本上是同龄人，感觉我们都在分享素材和故事，我们都说同一种语言，我觉得这才是最关键的。

00:03:42.821 --> 00:03:43.939
It's really interesting.
这真的很有趣。

00:03:43.939 --> 00:03:47.415
Okay, so return of the shirt is of course a dating episode.
好吧，所以“衬衫归来”当然是一集关于约会的剧集。

00:03:47.415 --> 00:03:49.300
It's not a relationship episode.
它不是一集关于恋爱的剧集。

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.513
It's like dating hell return of the shirt.
这就好像是约会地狱，“衬衫归来”。

00:03:51.513 --> 00:04:03.686
You were like talk about do you remember because I don't where the idea originally came from of like that idea of going back to that person going back to that a person who's stuck in the back of your head and you go, wait a second.
你当时说，聊聊——你还记得吗，因为我记不清了——最初那个想法是从哪来的，就是那种回到那个人身边、回到那个一直卡在你脑海深处的人身边，然后你想，等一下。

00:04:03.686 --> 00:04:05.109
Why don't I try that again?
我为什么不再试一次呢？

00:04:05.109 --> 00:04:13.331
Like, was that from, I forget if that was from your life or that was the room, just that specific thing of, I'm going to excavate back and find this person.
比如，那是源于，我忘了那是来自你的生活还是编剧室的构思，就是那种具体的情节：我要去深挖过去，把这个人找出来。

00:04:13.331 --> 00:04:15.017
Maybe that was the right person.
也许那才是对的人。

00:04:15.017 --> 00:04:16.545
Did that come from your life?
这源于你的生活吗？

00:04:16.545 --> 00:04:17.045
I forget.
我忘了。

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:28.439
I think there were like two sort of fundamental truths that we got to that I think I might have brought to the room or that we sort of like build on or maybe somebody else did.
我觉得大概有两个某种根本性的真相，可能是被我带到了编剧室，或者我们在此基础上进行了延伸，又或者其他人也有贡献。

00:04:28.439 --> 00:04:36.420
I'm I'm not exactly sure, but the idea of it really being hard to break up with someone, rang, always rang very true to me.
我记不太清了，但“和某人分手真的很艰难”这个想法，一直让我觉得非常真实。

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:40.339
And I'm kind of like a path of least resistance person.
我这人有点倾向于走阻力最小的路。

00:04:40.339 --> 00:04:47.220
And so I would just sort of like, ah, it's just easier to go along with this.
所以我通常会觉得，啊，顺其自然反而更容易。

00:04:47.579 --> 00:04:53.519
And so I think like that was one of the germs of the idea of like, oh, like it's hard to break up with people.
所以我觉得这算是那种萌芽的想法之一，比如，哦，跟别人分手太难了。

00:04:53.519 --> 00:05:00.004
And also the idea that it's so easy look back and reminisce and be like, hey, that person was great.
还有那种想法，就是回首过去和追忆往事是如此轻松，会觉得：嘿，那个人真棒。

00:05:00.004 --> 00:05:02.792
Like, maybe, maybe there's something there.
比如，也许，也许事情还有转机。

00:05:02.792 --> 00:05:06.683
Like that's, you know, like, well, why didn't that work out?
就像那种，你知道的，嗯，为什么当时没有成呢？

00:05:06.683 --> 00:05:16.410
And you sort of like, you know, you, you, you, you talk yourself into things, you know, and I think it's a little bit like wanting to rewrite history.
然后你就会，你知道的，你、你、你会说服自己接受某些事情，你知道的，我觉得这有点像想要改写历史。

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:32.751
It's a little bit the romantic idea of like, I've changed, you know, and so I think it was like, I think that's what I loved about the rewatch of this episode, I think it's grounded and there's these two very universal ideas that we all fall victim to.
这有点像那种浪漫的想法，比如“我已经变了”，你知道的，所以我觉得，这就是我喜欢重温这一集的地方，我觉得它很接地气，里面包含着两个我们所有人都会沦陷的普世观点。

00:05:32.751 --> 00:05:54.540
Yeah, it makes me think one thing about the agony of breakups and it's agonizing to be broken up with, but I have found it no less agonizing to break up with someone, especially if you care about them obviously, you've spent some significant time with them presumably, But also, like, I just don't like hurting people's feelings, right?
是的，这让我想起关于分手痛苦的一点，被甩固然痛苦，但我发现主动跟别人分手也同样痛苦，特别是如果你显然在乎他们的话，你可能和他们共度了相当长的一段时间，而且，我也只是不喜欢伤害别人的感情，对吧？

00:05:54.540 --> 00:06:05.368
Like, I avoid it at all costs, and there's something about the inability to have hard conversations, actually makes things much worse.
比如，我竭力避免这样做，而某种无法进行艰难对话的无能，实际上会让事情变得更糟。

00:06:05.368 --> 00:06:10.540
You end up making much more terrible decisions that hurt people.
你最终会做出更多伤害别人的糟糕决定。

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:15.339
And I also love that idea of the kind of, like, euphoric recall of a relationship.
我也很喜欢那种对一段感情的“欣快回忆”的想法。

00:06:15.339 --> 00:06:17.658
Like, you don't remember why you broke up with her.
比如，你不记得当初为什么要和她分手了。

00:06:17.658 --> 00:06:22.750
You just, it's like, the highlight real of relationship comes back to you and you're like, they were delightful.
你只是，这就好像，感情的精彩集锦回到了你的脑海里，你会觉得：他们太美好了。

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:23.660
What was my problem?
我当时有什么毛病？

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:25.816
And then you think it must have been me.
然后你会觉得一定是我自己的问题。

00:06:25.816 --> 00:06:27.003
It couldn't have been.
绝对不可能是对方的问题。

00:06:27.003 --> 00:06:29.537
And I've tried to revisit some of those things.
我也曾尝试重温过其中一些事情。

00:06:29.537 --> 00:06:31.801
And you go in with the best of intentions.
你怀着最好的意图去面对。

00:06:31.801 --> 00:06:38.379
And it's astonishing how quickly you get back to the place where you're like, oh, yeah, this was never, this wasn't right.
但惊人的是，你很快就会回到那种状态，你会觉得：哦，对了，这根本不对，这从来就不合适。

00:06:39.259 --> 00:06:40.536
It's not that they're a bad person.
并不是说他们是个坏人。

00:06:40.536 --> 00:06:41.740
It's not that I'm the bad person.
并不是说我是个坏人。

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:44.214
In the episode, it is astonishingly fast.
在这一集里，这种转变快得惊人。

00:06:44.214 --> 00:06:45.180
It's very tough.
这真的很难。

00:06:45.180 --> 00:07:05.610
It's very it's like I almost wish as every time you watch an episode now the my wife and I said we're rewatching it I go I wish we had five more minutes in this episode you know you think I was 25 and said a 22 because I'm like That felt very fast, but I guess that was also kind of the joke just like the second he gets her back He says I've got to get out of this which is funny.
这真的很——就好像我几乎希望，现在每次看一集的时候，我和我妻子说我们在重看，我都会想：我希望这集能再多给我们五分钟，你知道的，你可能会想我当时25岁却演了个22岁的，因为我觉得这进展太快了，但我想这也是笑点所在，就像他刚把她追回来说的那样：“我得赶紧离开这儿，”这挺搞笑的。

00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:06.253
It is funny.
这确实挺搞笑的。

00:07:06.253 --> 00:07:09.899
It's funny, but it's also brutal It's like yeah, what are you doing?
搞笑是搞笑，但这也太残酷了。

00:07:09.899 --> 00:07:15.289
Yeah, yeah, and you know why you don't know he doesn't ever show his work That's it.
就像是，天哪，你在干什么？

00:07:15.289 --> 00:07:17.085
No, that was my that was it.
对啊，对啊，而且你知道为什么吗？你不知道，他从来不展示他的心路历程，就是这样。

00:07:17.085 --> 00:07:29.660
He's just he never even explains why it's really is he just it's enough Oh, yeah, so Courtney also do you remember like where the the shirt that kind of talismanic object of the episode came from?
不，那就是我的，这就是全部。他甚至从不解释原因，真的就是——这就足够了。哦，对了，考妮，你还记得这集的那个衬衫——那种堪称护身符的物件——是从哪来的吗？

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:41.509
I was I can't remember I couldn't remember that I feel like that was your pitch I feel like that like that idea of going back in your like the shirt is this key that unlocks a door into the past that leads to her right?
我是……我记不清了，我记不得了，我觉得那是你的点子，我觉得那种回到过去的设想，就好像这件衬衫是打开通往过去大门的钥匙，能引领你找到她，对吧？

00:07:41.509 --> 00:07:42.483
Like I don't know.
就像我不知道一样。

00:07:42.483 --> 00:07:43.565
How do we get there?
我们是怎么做到的？

00:07:43.565 --> 00:07:45.404
I can't remember how we got there.
我记不得我们是怎么做到的了。

00:07:45.404 --> 00:07:53.574
I think I think it might have been a Carter thing that like the shirt that it like the shirt somehow connected it to the I don't I don't think I'm not.
我觉得，我觉得那可能是卡特（Carter）的点子，比如这件衬衫，这件衬衫不知怎么的把它和……我觉得，我觉得不是，我不是。

00:07:53.574 --> 00:07:55.901
That feels like it could be a Carter thing.
感觉这挺像卡特会有的点子。

00:07:55.901 --> 00:07:59.526
Like Carter we come in with a new shirt and have a new personality.
就像卡特换了件新衬衫就换了个新性格一样。

00:07:59.526 --> 00:08:00.230
Be like yeah.
就好像，对。

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:00.730
Yeah.
对。

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:01.230
Yeah.
对。

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:02.853
I maybe it was a a room thing.
我，也许这是编剧室里的某个点子。

00:08:02.853 --> 00:08:04.909
To be honest, I can't really remember.
说实话，我真的记不清了。

00:08:04.909 --> 00:08:05.883
But you know what?
但你知道吗？

00:08:05.883 --> 00:08:06.424
What's in.
有什么……

00:08:06.424 --> 00:08:07.398
Oh, go please, go.
哦，请说，请说。

00:08:07.398 --> 00:08:14.918
No, I'm just going to say that's a good portrait of like what it's like to me in a writer's room because sometimes you know what you pitch.
不，我只是想说，这很好地描绘了编剧室的工作状态，因为有时你知道什么是你提出的点子。

00:08:14.918 --> 00:08:21.898
And sometimes it's just like this hive mind kind of if we ask that entire room whose idea it was, it might be like, I don't know.
有时它就像是一种集体思维，如果我们问整个编剧室那是谁的主意，大家可能会说：“我不知道。”

00:08:21.898 --> 00:08:22.398
Yeah.
对。

00:08:22.398 --> 00:08:22.898
Totally.
完全是这样。

00:08:22.898 --> 00:08:25.658
It's just an interesting writer's room observation.
这真是一个有趣的编剧室现象。

00:08:25.658 --> 00:08:26.415
We keep going.
我们继续吧。

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:26.389
I got you off.
我打断你了。

00:08:26.389 --> 00:08:32.273
