00:54:53.920 --> 00:55:35.523
But I think Jordan has a question around creativity like I don't know if you guys are the same way, but I always have so many tabs open Like I have so many projects that are kind of in various stages of completion or disarray and Sometimes something is just sitting there half-formed and it just won't catch fire and other times something that was sitting there I'll be re-inspired and it'll just the heat will be there and I'll and I'll be able to finish it and maybe make it and I think sometimes like I try to honor honor the piece of art or the script or the song as having its own kind of arc and intelligence and knowledge about when it's going to be ready.
但我觉得乔丹提出的问题是关于创造力的，我不知道你们是不是也这样，但我总是开着太多网页标签，也就是说，我有太多项目处于不同的完成阶段或混乱状态，有时候某个东西就那么半成形地摆在那里，就是无法擦出火花；而有时候，对于那些放在那里的东西，我会重新获得灵感，热情就会涌现，然后我就能把它完成并创作出来。所以我觉得有时候，我试着去尊重一件艺术品、一个剧本或一首歌，把它当成有自己独特的弧光、智慧以及对何时准备就绪的自我认知的生命。

00:55:34.590 --> 00:55:41.570
So I really try to be in dialogue with the thing like, are you ready to open up to me, or am I ready to put the work in on this?
所以我真的很努力地与这个作品进行对话，比如，你准备好向我敞开了吗，或者我准备好为之付出努力了吗？

00:55:41.570 --> 00:55:44.210
So I've had things that have sat around for a while.
所以有些东西我搁置了很久。

00:55:44.210 --> 00:55:46.969
I just finished the last song from my album that I'm recording now.
我刚刚完成了现在正在录制的专辑里的最后一首歌。

00:55:46.969 --> 00:55:49.610
I started it on my honeymoon in April.
我在四月份的蜜月期间开始创作它。

00:55:49.610 --> 00:55:59.949
And it was just I would write like a first a month, you know, like, and other songs you sit down and they're, they're done in 20 minutes, like, but I, I, I really try to honor the thing and let it be.
当时的情况就是，我可能一个月才写出第一段，你知道的，而有些歌你一坐下来，20分钟就写完了，但我，我，我真的很尊重这个创作本身，顺其自然。

00:55:59.949 --> 00:56:05.389
But I, I don't, I don't throw anything away because I feel like there might be a day where it calls me back.
不过我，我，我从不扔掉任何东西，因为我觉得总有一天它会呼唤我回去。

00:56:05.389 --> 00:56:08.429
And the debate is always, is it a good idea to go back?
而争论的焦点总是，回去找它真的是个好主意吗？

00:56:08.429 --> 00:56:14.070
It's very much from the show because sometimes you go back to an idea and you have 10 to the moment Ted has of, dear God, I've got to get out of this.
这非常符合剧中的情节，因为有时你重新审视一个想法时，你会经历泰德那种“天啊，我得赶紧离开这儿”的时刻。

00:56:14.070 --> 00:56:15.070
Why did I go that totally?
我为什么会完全走那条路？

00:56:15.070 --> 00:56:20.313
Or you remember, you remember why you got stuck and threw it away or stopped working on it.
或者你会记起来，记起你当初为什么卡壳并把它扔掉或停止创作。

00:56:20.313 --> 00:56:23.713
Yeah, you know, that would happen all the time in the room.
对，你知道的，在编剧室里这种事经常发生。

00:56:23.713 --> 00:56:28.668
Like, we would be like, oh, we had an idea about, you know, the gang goes to the mall.
比如，我们会说，哦，我们有个点子，关于，你知道的，大家一起去商场。

00:56:28.668 --> 00:56:31.031
Oh, yeah, there was all this great stuff.
哦，对，有所有这些超棒的内容。

00:56:31.031 --> 00:56:32.471
There was all this stuff.
有所有这些内容。

00:56:32.471 --> 00:56:38.637
And then you pull up the notes and it was like, the gang goes to the mall and was like, that's what we had.
然后你翻开笔记一看，上面写着：大家一起去商场，然后就没有了，这就是我们当时所有的内容。

00:56:38.637 --> 00:56:39.328
Was it more?
还有别的吗？

00:56:39.328 --> 00:56:41.229
You remember it sort of more fun.
你记忆中的感觉要有趣得多。

00:56:41.230 --> 00:56:47.030
Yeah, we were ready to shrink that mall thing down to just a fake pop song by a Canadian pop Yeah, it's true.
对，我们当时准备把那个商场的情节缩减成一个加拿大流行歌手创作的虚构流行歌曲，对，确实如此。

00:56:47.030 --> 00:56:58.030
That feeling of going back to the room notes of something, and you go, like, that was excellent.
那种重新翻看以前的房间笔记的感觉，然后你会说，太棒了。

00:56:58.030 --> 00:56:59.030
That was cool.
这太酷了。

00:56:59.030 --> 00:57:00.030
This is going to catch fire.
这绝对能火。

00:57:00.030 --> 00:57:01.030
And you sit there for a whole day.
然后你在那里坐了一整天。

00:57:01.030 --> 00:57:03.030
And it's basically, I've got to get out of this.
基本上就是：我得赶紧离开这儿。

00:57:03.030 --> 00:57:04.030
I've got to.
我必须离开。

00:57:04.030 --> 00:57:05.030
And there isn't energy to these things.
这些东西根本没有能量。

00:57:05.030 --> 00:57:06.030
There isn't energy.
没有能量。

00:57:06.030 --> 00:57:07.030
And things have a time.
每样东西都有它的时候。

00:57:07.030 --> 00:57:08.030
I agree completely, Josh.
我完全同意，乔什。

00:57:08.030 --> 00:57:12.030
I've gotten very, like, woo, woo, about creative stuff, way of the two.
关于创作方面的东西，我也变得非常玄学，顺其自然。

00:57:12.030 --> 00:57:15.030
It's like, there's a time you shouldn't have to force it too much.
这就好像，时机到了，你就不应该强求太多。

00:57:15.030 --> 00:57:17.829
If something calls you back, you have to be honest.
如果有什么东西再次召唤你，你必须诚实面对。

00:57:17.829 --> 00:57:20.070
Do you want to go back to that thing?
你真的想回到那个东西上去吗？

00:57:20.070 --> 00:57:22.510
And Ted was not, it was yet again in this episode.
而泰德并没有，在本集中他又一次面临这种情况。

00:57:22.510 --> 00:57:24.110
Ted was not honest with himself.
泰德对自己的内心不够诚实。

00:57:24.110 --> 00:57:29.150
He just wanted the shortcut to feeling like, and she'll be the one, and she'll be answering.
他只是想要找到捷径，去感觉“就是她了，她会给出回应”。

00:57:29.150 --> 00:57:32.389
He's rushing, and he was not honest with himself about it.
他在急于求成，而且他对此并没有对自己坦诚。

00:57:32.389 --> 00:57:39.630
And I think there is a parallel between relationships and art where, I think there's this very American idea of like, relationships are work.
而且我认为感情和艺术之间存在一种相似之处，我觉得有一种非常美国式的观念，那就是：感情就是工作。

00:57:39.630 --> 00:57:41.909
They require work and art requires work.
它们需要付出努力，艺术也需要付出努力。

00:57:41.909 --> 00:57:44.550
And you got a grind away and grind away and work, work, work.
你必须不断地苦干、苦干，不停地工作、工作、工作。

00:57:44.550 --> 00:57:50.389
And I actually think that effortlessness is undervalued in both relationships and art.
但实际上我认为，在感情和艺术中，“毫不费力”这种特质都被低估了。

00:57:50.389 --> 00:58:01.510
Like, we've all caught a wave, where writing just feels like our fingers are being moved rather than we're moving them, where you really feel like something's just, you're capturing it, it's just coming through you.
比如，我们都曾有过那种状态，写作时感觉手指仿佛是在被推动着，而不是我们在移动它们，你会真切地觉得有什么东西正在涌现，你正在捕捉它，它只是通过你流淌出来。

00:58:01.510 --> 00:58:07.789
And in the same way, I struggled in relationships for so long and it really did feel like work.
同样地，我在感情中挣扎了那么久，那真的感觉像是在工作。

00:58:07.789 --> 00:58:10.309
And then I still have work with my wife.
然后我和我妻子之间也依然有需要磨合的地方。

00:58:10.309 --> 00:58:16.753
It's still, you still have to put forth the effort, you have to apologize when you screwed up all these things.
依然如此，你还是得付出努力，搞砸了的时候你得道歉，诸如此类。

00:58:16.753 --> 00:58:29.349
But there is a kind of ease and effortlessness when I, when I'm with her that feels new and different and like kind of what I was looking for without being able to even say that at the time because I didn't know that.
但和她在一起时，有一种自然而然的轻松感和毫不费力的默契，这感觉很新鲜、很不一样，就像是我一直在寻找的，只是当时连自己都说不清楚，因为我并不知道。

00:58:29.349 --> 00:58:32.577
Well, Josh, Jordan never gets upset at you.
好吧，乔什，乔丹可从来没生过你的气。

00:58:32.577 --> 00:58:41.434
She now knows she can go watch a lot of footage of you getting the shit kicked out of you, starting with episode four.
她现在知道自己可以去看很多关于你被打得落花流水的视频了，从第四集开始。

00:58:41.434 --> 00:58:43.085
We're trying to shirt.
我们正打算穿衬衫。

00:58:43.085 --> 00:58:46.087
She's like, I'm a little pissed at Josh.
她说：“我有点生乔什的气。”

00:58:46.087 --> 00:58:51.641
I'm like, why do you have a montage of Ted most beginning to be beaten up.
我说：“你为什么会有一段泰德挨揍的混剪视频呢？”

00:58:51.641 --> 00:58:58.321
So after our launch yesterday, we received incredible messages from all around the world.
所以昨天节目上线后，我们收到了来自世界各地不可思议的留言。

00:58:58.321 --> 00:59:09.580
They came to us from my newsletter or sub-stack page, from the Oomium sub-stack page, from Instagram at how we made your mother, and from our website.
它们来自我的通讯稿或Substack页面、Oomium的Substack页面、Instagram上的“how we made your mother”账号，以及我们的网站。

00:59:09.579 --> 00:59:17.204
And we're reading them all, and we're so grateful to everyone for taking the time to tell us what how I met your mother means to you.
我们正在阅读所有的留言，非常感谢大家抽出时间来告诉我们《老爸老妈浪漫史》对你们意味着什么。

00:59:17.204 --> 00:59:22.421
This one is from Amy, and it was a response to the sub-stack that I wrote on my newsletter.
这条留言来自艾米，它是对我在个人通讯稿上写的Substack文章的回应。

00:59:22.421 --> 00:59:27.752
And she writes, the show came out the year after I first got married and had my second child.
她写道，这部剧在我结婚并有了第二个孩子的第二年开播。

00:59:27.752 --> 00:59:30.046
But it took a few years for me to watch.
但我过了几年才开始看。

00:59:30.046 --> 00:59:33.141
First watch was when Netflix still did Mail DVDs, LOL.
第一次看的时候，Netflix还提供邮寄DVD的服务，哈哈。

00:59:33.141 --> 00:59:38.473
The characters were the exact age I was, slash AM in all the years that happened, loved that.
剧中的角色和我当时（或者说在剧集发生的那些年里）的年龄一模一样，太喜欢这一点了。

00:59:38.473 --> 00:59:49.365
In my late teens, early 20s, I was a friends fan, watched with my mom, and even got to go to set there at one point, but they were 10 years older than me, so hymnium solidified more with me.
在我十几岁到二十出头的时候，我是《老友记》的粉丝，和妈妈一起看，甚至还去过那部剧的片场，但他们比我大10岁，所以《老爸老妈浪漫史》更能引起我的共鸣。

00:59:49.365 --> 00:59:51.716
There's so much realism philosophy in it.
里面有那么多的现实主义哲学。

00:59:51.716 --> 00:59:55.442
My best friend, who's 10 years younger than me, loves it as well.
我最好的朋友比我小10岁，她也很喜欢这部剧。

00:59:55.442 --> 01:00:00.831
And one example of the greatness slash realism, she often brings up is the one curly fry, LOL.
她经常提起的一个展现其伟大之处或现实主义的例子，就是那根卷曲的薯条，哈哈。

01:00:00.831 --> 01:00:08.570
We both are huge romantics like Ted, but we've been through some rough relationships and had to realize we had some codependent issues.
我们俩都和泰德一样是超级浪漫主义者，但我们都经历过一些糟糕的感情，不得不意识到自己有一些共情依赖的问题。

01:00:08.570 --> 01:00:13.787
And other things we've worked out in therapy and Coda groups shout out to Coda and therapy.
以及我们在心理治疗和CoDA（匿名共依存症互助会）小组中解决的其他问题，向CoDA和心理治疗致敬。

01:00:13.787 --> 01:00:20.380
So that has changed some perspective on the characters and things on the show but doesn't make us love it any less.
所以这改变了我们对剧中角色和一些事情的看法，但并没有减少我们对它的喜爱。

01:00:21.019 --> 01:00:22.271
The show is cathartic.
这部剧具有宣泄情绪的作用。

01:00:22.271 --> 01:00:23.694
It's a night watch still.
它依然是深夜必看的节目。

01:00:23.694 --> 01:00:31.659
Sometimes I go from the first episode to the last but often I let Alexa or Siri choose a number of the season and for the season in episode.
有时我从第一集看到最后一集，但通常我会让Alexa或Siri随机选择一个季数以及具体的某一集。

01:00:31.659 --> 01:00:35.980
Sometimes I go to a specific episode if I need to think back on something or process some feelings.
有时如果我需要回想某些事情或处理一些情绪，我会去看特定的一集。

01:00:35.980 --> 01:00:39.340
I choreographed a yellow umbrella ballet a lobby on Rose last year.
我去年在露丝街的大厅里编排过一出黄伞芭蕾。

01:00:39.340 --> 01:00:40.219
Oh my god.
天呐。

01:00:40.219 --> 01:00:41.095
I've danced to your songs.
我跳过你们的歌。

01:00:41.095 --> 01:00:42.139
Can you send up a clip of that?
你能发一段那个的片段过来吗？

01:00:42.139 --> 01:00:42.639
Please send us a clip of that.
请发一段那个的片段给我们吧。

01:00:42.639 --> 01:00:43.139
Please send us a clip of that.
请发一段那个的片段给我们吧。

01:00:43.019 --> 01:00:44.059
Please send us a clip of that.
请发一段那个的片段给我们吧。

01:00:44.059 --> 01:00:46.860
I've danced to your songs and many other pivotal songs from the show.
我跳过你们的歌，还有剧中许多其他关键歌曲。

01:00:46.860 --> 01:00:51.340
I have pop culture, him, you reminders, peppered throughout my house, shower curtains, earrings, pictures.
我的家里到处都是流行文化、他、你的纪念品，比如浴帘、耳环、照片。

01:00:51.340 --> 01:01:01.900
I never don't cry a **** for the **** Robyn saying **** the speech Ted gives **** before **** because so often I've wanted to time travel or skip ahead to adjust things.
每次看到罗宾说脏话、泰德在……之前发表的演讲，我都忍不住落泪，因为我常常想时光倒流或快进以调整一些事情。

01:01:01.900 --> 01:01:03.739
Though ultimately they happen as they should.
尽管它们最终都会按照应有的方式发生。

01:01:04.380 --> 01:01:05.980
All just so relatable.
所有这一切都太有共鸣了。

01:01:05.980 --> 01:01:10.059
I've always been fascinated with the subject of fate, destiny, free will.
我一直对命运、天意和自由意志这些主题很着迷。

01:01:10.059 --> 01:01:15.500
There's private reasons about who I watched the show with that also tie me to my love of the show.
因为我和谁一起看这部剧，还有一些个人的原因，这也将我对这部剧的喜爱紧密连结在一起。

01:01:15.500 --> 01:01:21.352
It just is different than any other show, and we'll always have a huge place of comfort and love in my heart.
它就是与众不同，超越了其他任何剧集，并永远在我心中占据着一个充满温暖与爱的重要位置。

01:01:21.352 --> 01:01:22.157
Thank you, Amy.
谢谢你，艾米。

01:01:22.157 --> 01:01:22.694
That's so.
太……

01:01:22.694 --> 01:01:23.500
Thank you, Amy.
谢谢你，艾米。

01:01:23.500 --> 01:01:24.000
Lovely.
太美好了。

01:01:24.000 --> 01:01:24.548
So nice.
太棒了。

01:01:24.548 --> 01:01:29.759
And honestly, please do send us the footage of that dance you were going on.
说实话，请务必把刚才提到的那段舞蹈素材发给我们。

01:01:29.739 --> 01:01:31.338
Yeah, I'd be really happy.
对，我真的很乐意看。

